WEBVTT

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.560
A mission calendar coordinates requests, approvals, crews, aircraft, payloads, locations,

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.245
weather windows, maintenance, and deliverable deadlines.

00:00:07.245 --> 00:00:11.630
A scheduled slot is provisional until current conditions and readiness support a final go

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:12.820
decision.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.280
Location and airspace review uses current official sources and site information.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.365
The plan should address permissions, restrictions, terrain, obstacles, people, property,

00:00:20.365 --> 00:00:24.000
launch and recovery, communications, and emergency landing options.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.957
Weather checks begin during scheduling and continue through launch.

00:00:26.957 --> 00:00:29.775
Wind, visibility, precipitation, temperature, lighting,

00:00:29.775 --> 00:00:32.160
and rapid change affect safety and data quality.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.435
Define thresholds and alternate dates before urgency increases.

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:39.430
Crew scheduling considers qualifications, currency, fatigue, travel, duty time,

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.560
site familiarity, and role coverage.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:45.640
A payload specialist or observer may be necessary even when one pilot could technically

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.295
operate the aircraft.

00:00:47.295 --> 00:00:51.280
Aircraft readiness includes maintenance status, battery condition, firmware configuration,

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.585
payload compatibility, storage, spares, and required documents.

00:00:54.585 --> 00:00:58.320
Assigning an available aircraft is not enough; it must be suitable for the mission.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.075
The final plan documents objective, route, altitude strategy, collection settings, risks,

00:01:03.075 --> 00:01:07.100
controls, communications, battery margins, emergency procedures, data handling,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.580
and abort criteria.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:12.455
Contingencies should simplify decisions rather than create a complicated alternate

00:01:12.455 --> 00:01:13.080
mission.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.520
Scheduling conflicts need transparent priority rules.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.240
Safety, emergency need, authorization, weather window, asset suitability,

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.031
and business consequence should outweigh seniority or who asked most loudly.

00:01:23.031 --> 00:01:24.920
Record why a mission displaced another.
